==English==
{{was wotd|2017|April|1}}
[[File:StonyStratford CockandBull.jpg|thumb|[[pub|Pub]] signs of The Cock and The Bull in {{w|Stony Stratford}}, [[Milton Keynes]], [[Buckinghamshire]], [[England]], UK]]

===Etymology===
{{unk|en|title=Origin unknown}}. Folk history claims derivation from the rivalry between two inns in {{w|Stony Stratford}}, [[Buckinghamshire]], [[England]], one called “The Cock” and the other called “The Bull”, where travellers would congregate to hear fanciful stories told; one such story involved travellers destined for the city of {{w|Banbury}}. However, there is little evidence supporting this etymology.<ref>{{R:Phrase Finder|entry=A cock and bull story|url=http://www.phrases.org.uk/meanings/cock-and-bull-story.html|accessdate=28 December 2016}}</ref>

